{"answerable":true,"answer":"No. Travelling to a place your employer tells you to go to pick up a truck that falls under the rules is not your free time, because you are meeting an obligation to your employer. This applies even when your boss leaves it to you to decide when and how to travel. The time must be recorded as 'availability' or 'other work', depending on your country's national law. If you drive a vehicle that is outside the scope of the Regulation to get there, that time counts as 'other work'. This covers places that are not your home or the employer's operational centre. If the other depot is itself your employer's operational centre, the passages do not say how to treat it.

There is one main exception. If you travel by train or ferry to or from the pick-up point and have access to a bunk or couchette, that time can count as rest or a break.

Note: this guidance dates from 2007, before the 2020 Mobility Package changed the rules, and the current text of Article 9 is not among the passages.","citations":[{"node_id":"guidance-2:p1","quote":"A driver travelling to a specific place, other than the employer's operating centre, indicated to him/her by the employer in order to take over and drive a 'tachograph vehicle' is satisfying an obligation towards his/her employer and therefore he/she does not freely dispose of his/her time."},{"node_id":"guidance-2:p1","quote":"regardless of whether the employer gave instructions as to when and how to travel or whether that choice was left to the driver, should be recorded as either 'availability' or 'other work' depending on the Member State's national legislation"},{"node_id":"guidance-2:p1","quote":"any time spent by a driver travelling to or from a location, which is not the driver's home or the employer's operational centre"},{"node_id":"guidance-2:p1","quote":"any time spent by a driver driving a vehicle, which is out of scope of the Regulation, to or from a location, which is not the driver's home or the employer's operational centre and where a driver is supposed to take over or to leave a vehicle which falls within the scope of the Regulation should be recorded as 'other work'"},{"node_id":"guidance-2:p1","quote":"The second case is when a driver is not accompanying a vehicle, but is travelling by train or ferry to or from a place where he/she will take charge or has relinquished control of a vehicle within the scope of the Regulation (Art. 9.2) provided that he/she has an access to a couchette or bunk on that ferry or train."}]}
